proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "button_location",
      "description": "After the requested repetitions, the stored button center systematically placed the button waypoints too far toward negative world x. The previous annotation localized the raised circular surface at z≈0.02 m; in several scenes this produced more than 1 cm disagreement with the recorded button alignment. Requiring that inaccurate center for every button action broke SUFFICIENCY. Keep this field as the stationary button's world xy center, but localize the raised circular top at approximately z=0.03 m, rather than z=0.02 m. Use the calibrated visible circular surface, not the base or tabletop, and cross-check against the wrist view when available. Initialize to [] at the video cycle; populate at the first clearly visible calibrated execution view and retain thereafter through occlusion and contact. Recompute the annotations using this corrected surface height rather than adding an episode-specific offset. An example corrected value is approximately [-0.169, -0.028].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "button_location",
      "description": "The button approach and press failed because the stored center, estimated using a surface height of 0.02 m, was systematically displaced toward negative world x; blindly retaining that estimate made the required button waypoints inconsistent with the memory. This is a SUFFICIENCY failure. Initialize button_location to [] in the video cycle. At the first execution cycle with calibration and a clearly visible button, intersect the viewing ray through the center of its raised circular top with approximately world z=0.03 m and store world [x,y]. Use the circular top rather than its base or the tabletop; cross-check with the current calibrated wrist view when available. Retain the resulting stationary center through subsequent manipulation, occlusion, and button contact. Do not apply an episode-specific xy offset. An example value is approximately [-0.169, -0.028]."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "After the requested pick-up-and-put-down repetitions and the final empty-hand retreat, the policy approached, closed, pressed, and retracted at an inaccurately stored button center. The old rule required identical stored xy throughout, producing errors exceeding 1 cm toward negative world x. This broke SUFFICIENCY, not repetition counting or target tracking. Replace the button localization reference of z≈0.02 m with the corrected raised-top height of approximately z=0.03 m specified in the memory instruction. Approach the corrected stored center at z=0.15 with the gripper open, roll=-pi, pitch=0, yaw=0. Remove the blanket requirement to return to stored xy during every subsequent button action. Once the open hand has reached above the button, use the current calibrated wrist view to check circular-top alignment; close the empty gripper near the achieved TCP xy, making only a small visually justified centering correction. Command z=0.15 unless at least 2 mm displacement requires a small upward offset. Once closed and aligned above the button, descend essentially vertically at the achieved alignment to commanded z=0.0075, keeping closed, rather than moving back to an older stored estimate. If the button is obscured, preserve the achieved alignment instead of guessing a new center. After the current motion shows downward button contact, retract vertically from the reported current TCP xy to z=0.15, keeping closed and using the downward, yaw-zero orientation. Actual contact around z=0.0245 is sufficient evidence to retract, not a reason to push deeper. Retain the existing repetition, release, and clearance rules."
}

